**Answer: Yes.** A compact Lie group can have a non-compact Lie subgroup — provided the subgroup is *immersed* (necessarily non-closed). Every *closed* subgroup of a compact Lie group is compact, so any non-compact subgroup must fail to be closed.

**Construction.** Let $G = \mathbb{T}^2 = \mathbb{R}^2/\mathbb{Z}^2$ be the 2-torus, which is compact. Fix an irrational number $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q}$, and define

$$\varphi : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{T}^2, \qquad \varphi(t) = (t,\, \alpha t) \bmod \mathbb{Z}^2.$$

This is a Lie group homomorphism (a one-parameter subgroup). Its differential at $0$ is $v \mapsto (v, \alpha v)$, which is injective, so $\varphi$ is an immersion. Its kernel is

$$\ker\varphi = \{t \in \mathbb{R} : t \in \mathbb{Z},\; \alpha t \in \mathbb{Z}\}.$$

If $t \in \ker\varphi$ with $t \neq 0$, then $t = n \in \mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0\}$ and $\alpha n \in \mathbb{Z}$, i.e. $\alpha \in \mathbb{Q}$, contradicting irrationality. Hence $\ker\varphi = \{0\}$, so $\varphi$ is injective.

By the standard theorem on immersed subgroups (Chevalley's theorem), an injective immersion that is a group homomorphism endows its image $H := \varphi(\mathbb{R})$ with a unique Lie group structure making $\varphi : \mathbb{R} \to H$ a diffeomorphism. Thus $H$ is a Lie subgroup of $\mathbb{T}^2$ isomorphic to $\mathbb{R}$, which is **non-compact**.

**Why $H$ is not closed (and why this is forced).** Since $\alpha$ is irrational, the image $\{(t \bmod 1,\, \alpha t \bmod 1) : t \in \mathbb{R}\}$ is dense in $\mathbb{T}^2$ (Kronecker's theorem). A dense proper subgroup cannot be closed, so $H$ is a non-closed, immersed (not embedded) Lie subgroup.

This non-closedness is unavoidable: any *closed* subgroup $K$ of a compact topological group $G$ is compact (closed subset of a compact space), and by the closed-subgroup theorem it is an embedded Lie subgroup. Therefore:

- **Closed Lie subgroups** of a compact Lie group are always compact.
- **Non-closed (immersed) Lie subgroups** can be non-compact, as the example above shows.

**Conclusion.** A compact Lie group *can* have a non-compact Lie subgroup; such a subgroup is necessarily non-closed (indeed dense in some subtorus), and the irrational-slope one-parameter subgroup of $\mathbb{T}^2$ gives an explicit example isomorphic to $\mathbb{R}$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
